WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.843 --> 00:00:03.246
A planning project at a grid operator.

2
00:00:03.954 --> 00:00:07.194
You, the consultant, stand at the board with the sponsor,

3
00:00:07.426 --> 00:00:09.017
the team lead and the planner.

4
00:00:09.749 --> 00:00:13.975
Five tasks: the baseline analysis, designing the readiness check,

5
00:00:14.173 --> 00:00:18.143
training the planners, arranging system access for the pilot region,

6
00:00:18.341 --> 00:00:19.664
and the pilot itself.

7
00:00:20.654 --> 00:00:23.626
Some work can only start when other work is finished.

8
00:00:24.428 --> 00:00:25.821
That is a dependency.

9
00:00:26.507 --> 00:00:30.350
You can't pilot the new readiness check before the planners agree on it.

10
00:00:31.075 --> 00:00:33.908
On the board, each dependency becomes an arrow.

11
00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.003
How do you find them?

12
00:00:36.656 --> 00:00:40.360
Ask each owner: what do you need from someone else before you can start?

13
00:00:41.123 --> 00:00:44.594
The team lead can only train once the check is designed.

14
00:00:45.365 --> 00:00:49.231
And the pilot needs trained planners, and access to the system.

15
00:00:50.293 --> 00:00:55.308
At clients, most dependencies are not about tasks but about people and decisions:

16
00:00:55.494 --> 00:00:56.167
a sign-off,

17
00:00:56.411 --> 00:00:58.570
the works council, a production stop.

18
00:00:59.298 --> 00:01:00.854
Those go on the board too.

19
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.419
Now the durations.

20
00:01:04.166 --> 00:01:06.674
The baseline, A, takes 3 weeks.

21
00:01:07.502 --> 00:01:08.675
Then two chains.

22
00:01:09.446 --> 00:01:14.810
Designing the check, B, 4 weeks, followed by training the planners, D, 2 weeks.

23
00:01:15.535 --> 00:01:18.670
And arranging system access, C, 6 weeks.

24
00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:23.338
The pilot, E, takes 6 weeks and needs both D and C.

25
00:01:24.343 --> 00:01:27.025
Add up each chain, from start to finish.

26
00:01:27.746 --> 00:01:32.867
The longest chain of dependent work is the critical path, and it sets the end date.

27
00:01:33.616 --> 00:01:38.782
The method dates from 1957, from DuPont and Remington Rand.

28
00:01:39.843 --> 00:01:45.230
Chain A, B, D, E: 3 plus 4 plus 2 plus 6 is 15 weeks.

29
00:01:46.262 --> 00:01:51.335
And chain A, C, E: 3 plus 6 plus 6 is also 15 weeks.

30
00:01:52.059 --> 00:01:54.927
Two chains of 15 weeks: both are critical.

31
00:01:56.263 --> 00:02:00.640
If the system access slips a week, the pilot can only start a week later,

32
00:02:00.861 --> 00:02:02.649
and the end date moves a week.

33
00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:07.722
The same goes for the check and the training: that chain is critical too.

34
00:02:08.428 --> 00:02:10.738
So you watch the critical path closely.

35
00:02:11.424 --> 00:02:15.313
Tasks off that path may slip a bit; you don't panic about those.

36
00:02:16.338 --> 00:02:20.425
A typical failure: the plan shows only tasks the team controls.

37
00:02:21.134 --> 00:02:26.126
The slow outside steps are missing, like IT access, a supplier or a permit.

38
00:02:26.953 --> 00:02:29.600
These often turn out to be the critical path.

39
00:02:30.315 --> 00:02:31.314
Here that is C.

40
00:02:32.304 --> 00:02:36.333
If you need to go faster, only shortening the critical path helps.

41
00:02:37.058 --> 00:02:40.216
Shorten it too much and another chain becomes critical.

42
00:02:40.916 --> 00:02:44.399
With two critical chains, here you would have to shorten both.

43
00:02:45.713 --> 00:02:49.684
So you ask IT whether the access request can start in week 1,

44
00:02:49.858 --> 00:02:51.437
instead of after the baseline.

45
00:02:52.122 --> 00:02:54.746
It can: C now runs alongside A.

46
00:02:55.781 --> 00:03:01.761
The end date stays at 15 weeks, because A, B, D, E is still 15 weeks long.

47
00:03:02.755 --> 00:03:08.107
But access is now ready after week 6, and the pilot only starts after week 9.

48
00:03:08.806 --> 00:03:10.942
In between lie 3 weeks of slack:

49
00:03:11.139 --> 00:03:14.425
how much a task can slip without moving the end date.

50
00:03:15.492 --> 00:03:21.168
If IT is now two weeks late, the slack absorbs it, and the end date no longer moves.

51
00:03:22.532 --> 00:03:23.786
Then the milestones.

52
00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:27.348
A milestone is a moment, not an activity.

53
00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:32.098
Not the analysis phase, but the moment the readiness check is agreed,

54
00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:33.618
or the pilot starts.

55
00:03:34.315 --> 00:03:36.625
On the plan, a diamond, not a bar.

56
00:03:37.665 --> 00:03:42.576
The most important kind is a go/no-go: the decision to continue or roll out,

57
00:03:42.832 --> 00:03:45.884
that is, introduce it everywhere, or to stop.

58
00:03:46.626 --> 00:03:49.006
Here after the pilot, in week 15.

59
00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:55.311
Good milestones are decisions or visible results, about every three to six weeks.

60
00:03:56.014 --> 00:03:59.903
Here every six weeks: in week 3, 9 and 15.

61
00:04:00.968 --> 00:04:04.648
They give the steering group, the sponsor's regular decision meeting,

62
00:04:04.787 --> 00:04:07.875
something to check, and the team something to aim at.

63
00:04:09.247 --> 00:04:13.590
Put the plan on one page as a Gantt chart, named after Henry Gantt,

64
00:04:13.741 --> 00:04:16.760
who drew such charts in the 1910s.

65
00:04:17.603 --> 00:04:23.384
Deliverables as rows, weeks as columns, bars for the work, milestones as diamonds,

66
00:04:23.582 --> 00:04:24.778
and an owner per row.

67
00:04:25.804 --> 00:04:30.494
Check it against the client's calendar: shutdowns, holidays, peak season,

68
00:04:30.715 --> 00:04:32.491
and the steering group dates.

69
00:04:33.204 --> 00:04:36.443
If the pilot falls in a shutdown, you see it now.

70
00:04:37.441 --> 00:04:39.728
Hang it on the wall where the team works.

71
00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.228
A plan people see every day gets corrected.

72
00:04:43.972 --> 00:04:46.015
A plan in a file gets forgotten.

73
00:04:47.011 --> 00:04:49.031
Experts argue about software.

74
00:04:49.763 --> 00:04:53.513
A big tool like MS Project calculates the paths for you.

75
00:04:54.173 --> 00:04:57.133
But a wall plan gets more corrections from the floor.

76
00:04:58.161 --> 00:05:00.703
Ask one sharp question at every review:

77
00:05:00.831 --> 00:05:03.525
what has to happen this month for the end date to hold?
